whenever we think about the cause of  cancer  we very often think cancer is caused due  to mutagens such as  uv rays cigarette smoke car smoke etc  but many of us don't know that cancer  could also be caused  due to gene mutation or even by  viruses so in this video our focus  is how cancer could be caused by viruses  and what is the role of viruses in terms  of causing cancer  virus could be also a cancer-causing  agent came from the work of peyton  rouse in early 1918  and peyton rouse did a very simple  experiment  he was called in in a poultry farmer's  house to  analyze some chickens which are having  tumors in their legs  and that is known as sarcoma  so first virus to be detected  as cancer-causing agent was  rouse sarcoma virus and it was termed as  a oncovirus  now rao sarcoma virus called sarcoma in  chicken and it's a  retrovirus it has rna as a genetic  material  so we look at the functionality of it  and how it can cause cancer  so let us look at what experiment  exactly  was performed by him so he observed  that in chicken leg there is growth and  this is sarcoma  so he took a sample of this sarcoma he  cut a portion of this tissue  and homogenized that tissue nicely  after homogenization from the  homogeneity he filtered  this thing and cell-free filtrate  was then injected on a healthy chicken  and what he can show that after  injecting the filtrate  the healthy chicken also develops  sarcoma and his conclusion was  the agent or the causal agent that is  resulting in  sarcoma formation can be transmitted  from one organism to another organism  yeah by that time he didn't know that  this sarcoma is actually caused by a  virus  later on howard tenim developed an  acid where he  can transform chicken's embryonic  fibroblast  into an altered morphology using rao  sarcoma virus infection  so he has extracted cheek fibroblast and  put it in culture  now he can infect this culture with  rouse sarcoma virus and what he can see  these fibroblasts are transformed  in nature that means raw sarcoma virus  has a profound importance in terms of  transforming normal fibroblasts into  uh transformed fibroblast and now  rao's experiment makes sense  whatever he thought as an agent that can  cause  or that can be transmitted from one  individual to another individual was  actually this virus  now tenim also took the approach  of piton rouse he extracted  samples from the sarcoma he ground it up  he filtered it with specific filters  such that only biomolecule can remain  any other cellular components can go  away  and injecting that filter into a healthy  chicken develops sarcoma  in that particular chicken that means  viral derived elements are responsible  for this cancer so let us look at a  little bit about  rao sarcoma virus so rao sarcoma virus  have rna as a genetic material which can  eventually be converted  to a double-stranded cdna which  integrates  inside the host genome now using the  host genomic machinery  it is getting expressed and the major  proteins that are expressed from this  genome  is gag ball and envelope the major genes  that are encoding  proteins are gag pollen envelope now  from paul gene there are reverse  transcriptase integras and protease  these proteins are produced just like an  hiv virus but along with that  the disease-causing gene turned out to  be  sarc it was named as v sarc or viral  sarc  but this sarc has very similarity to a  cellular version of it or c sarc  now what is actually v sarc and c-ser so  let's look at it  so rao sarcoma viruses  rna was taken out and cdna was prepared  from that  now using that cdna derived from rao  sarcoma virus  chicken cdna library was hybridized  and they found out that there are  hybridization between  the rao sarcoma virus derived v sarc  and a particular portion  of the genome of chicken that means the  v  sarc gene is not exclusive to the virus  it is also present in the chicken and it  is present in many of these  animals in our animal kingdom so the  visa  the visa gene is essential for  proliferation of the virus but it it  increases the virulence property this  arc is nothing but a kinase  which regulates a bunch of pathway it  modulates  map kinase cascade wind signaling jack  stat signaling to name a few  so it is very important in terms of  regulating growth signaling pathway  and as a result you can imagine if the  src kinase is constitutively active all  of these signaling pathway are activated  for a very long time resulting in  uncontrolled and unchecked growth  which might be a leading cause of cancer  so indeed  the vsark has a short structure it  doesn't have a  short residue from the c terminal end  which  leads to its constitutive activation  that means vsarc  is always activated and it can cause  uncontrolled proliferation by activating  many  mitogenic pathways in the host cell  and it results in uncontrolled  proliferation which leads to cancer  so in this video what we learned about  is how  virus can cause cancer how it was  detected  that virus can be a cause of cancer an  exact  mechanism by which rouse sarcoma virus  can  lead to cancer we have learned in this  video so i hope you  enjoyed this video but before we end i  just wanted to let you know  that there are many other viruses such  as hepatitis viruses there are  papillomaviruses there are epstein-barr  viruses which can lead to  several type of cancer and now there are  hundreds of viruses which are discovered  in context of cancer  and the list is growing so  what we learned so far in this video is  mutagens are not the sole cause of  cancer it can also be caused due to  oncoviruses and it can also be caused  due to genetic reasons  so i hope you enjoyed this video if you  like this video give it a quick thumbs  up and don't forget to like share and  subscribe and do let me know in the  comment how do you like my videos  thank you thanks for listening
 

